<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saxo Grammaticus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1160–after 1208</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saxo Grammaticus was a Danish historian and cleric in the service of Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon of Lund</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Gesta Danorum, in sixteen books, traces Danish history from legendary kings to the 1180s and preserves many Scandinavian myths and legends, including the tale of Amleth, the source of Shakespeare's Hamlet. It also describes the Danish destruction of the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svantevit">Svantevit</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Arkona</a> in 1168.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saxo-grammaticus.jpg" alt="Saxo Grammaticus" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Moe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saxo_Grammaticus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Saxo is thought to have been born in Zealand, on the evidence of the Jutland Chronicle, which first gives him the name Grammaticus, meaning the learned. The Sjælland Chronicle calls him Saxo cognomine Longus, with the byname the tall. Sven Aggesen, a Danish nobleman who wrote a slightly earlier history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, describes him as his contubernalis, or tent-comrade, which suggests that both may have served in the royal guard, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hird">Hird</a>. Saxo writes that he comes from a warrior family: he follows the ancient right of hereditary service, and his father and grandfather frequented the war camp of Valdemar I. Whether he was a layman or a cleric is uncertain, but either way he was well educated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Saxo was in the retinue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a>, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> and the foremost adviser to Valdemar I, and received his patronage. In his will Absalon forgives his clerk Saxo a small debt of two and a half marks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and tells him to return two borrowed books to the monastery of Sorø. In the preface to his history Saxo writes that Absalon had encouraged him to write a heroic history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, to glorify the fatherland on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s Aeneid. The work is thought to have been begun about 1185. Saxo wrote the preface last, under Anders Sunesen, who replaced Absalon as archbishop, and in it he warmly praised both archbishops and the reigning king, Valdemar II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Gesta Danorum begins with the founders of the Danish people, Dan I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and Angul, and extends to about the year 1187. The first four books treat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> before Christ, the next four their history after Christ, books 9 to 12 Christian Denmark, and books 13 to 16 promote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> and exploits before and during Saxo's own lifetime. His sources were of questionable historical value: oral tales of the Icelanders, ancient volumes, letters carved on rocks and stone, and the statements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a>.</p><p>Saxo's world has very warlike values, and he glorifies heroes who made their names in battle far more than those who made peace. His gods are not always good characters. Odin teaches Harald, in Saxo's tale a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, the ways of war and then betrays and kills him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> praised Saxo's Latin, and early in the sixteenth century Christiern Pedersen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> printed the work with Jodocus Badius Ascensius.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saxo Grammaticus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Other sources are written by outsiders: the Danish historian <b>Saxo Grammaticus</b>, around 1200, retold Norse myths as history in Latin, including the story of Amleth, the original of Shakespeare’s Hamlet; the German chronicler Adam of Bremen described the temple at Uppsala around 1075; and the Arab envoy Ibn…</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>For the West Slavs between the Vistula and the Elbe, the chronicles of Thietmar of Merseburg, Adam of Bremen and Helmold, the lives of Otto of Bamberg, <b>Saxo Grammaticus</b>’ Gesta Danorum and the Knýtlinga saga describe temples and cults in detail.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rani were ruled by princes, but <b>Saxo Grammaticus</b> and Helmold of Bosau describe the high priest of Arkona as the real power.</blockquote><a href="/state/rani">Rani</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zealand, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.57,11.89&amp;year=1191" aria-label="Zealand on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4810 268 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saxo Grammaticus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5260 538h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5260 538h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.57,11.89&amp;year=1191">What was here in 1191? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Indeed, they account it a delight to learn and to consign to remembrance the history of all nations, deeming it as great a glory to set forth the excellences of others as to display their own.&#8221;</p><footer>Gesta Danorum (The Danish History), preface &#183; tr. Oliver Elton, 1894 &#183; on the Icelanders, whose records he followed &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1150/pg1150-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Men who though they lacked acquaintance with, the speech of Rome, were yet seized with such a passion for bequeathing some record of their history, that they encompassed huge boulders instead of scrolls, borrowing rocks for the usage of books.&#8221;</p><footer>Gesta Danorum (The Danish History), preface &#183; tr. Oliver Elton, 1894 &#183; on the runestones of the early Danes &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1150/pg1150-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…since this present work promises not a trumpery dazzle of language, but faithful information concerning times past.&#8221;</p><footer>Gesta Danorum (The Danish History), preface &#183; tr. Oliver Elton, 1894 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1150/pg1150-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;So he chose to feign dulness, and pretend an utter lack of wits. This cunning course not only concealed his intelligence but ensured his safety.&#8221;</p><footer>Gesta Danorum (The Danish History), book 3 &#183; tr. Oliver Elton, 1894 &#183; of the Danish prince Amleth, whose story Shakespeare's Hamlet retells &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1150/pg1150-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…and accordingly he mingled craft and candour in such wise that, though his words did lack truth, yet there was nothing to betoken the truth and betray how far his keenness went.&#8221;</p><footer>Gesta Danorum (The Danish History), book 3 &#183; tr. Oliver Elton, 1894 &#183; of the Danish prince Amleth, whose story Shakespeare's Hamlet retells &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1150/pg1150-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
